you know and and it's like no you you're not going to break the computer by typing commands on the keyboard and and we're used to things being complicated and technical and having a bunch of documentation you had to read that was, I think, more normal then than it is now. Today, we have, I think, more impatience, particularly with respect to getting started with something. One of the things, this actually dovetails with other stories about my career. At the University of Michigan, I fell in with a group at the Ann Arbor Computing Club, who included a bunch of the initial people at Project Xanadu. Xanadu was one of the sort of the original hypertext projects. In fact, it was founded by Ted Nelson, who is the person who actually coined the term hypertext. And they had this whole vision for this worldwide,
